nd i  guess a lyricism to the  way that that story is told visually uh  and and in the edit it was really about  trying to  it was a trying to find that calibration  between revealing too much and  just enough but not too much uh  particularly in in  the the transformation of sam sam's  character of naomi what's his character  if you you know it's frames here or  there in the end like if you go too  far the story feels like it's it's over  but if you hold it back  it's not moving fast enough so it was a  very delicate balance  um as well as you know trying to  make uh a character in penguin  like and what i really what one of the  things i was drawn to  with this story was having a main  character in the film who didn't speak  um and as a bird and trying to find ways  of  you know without anthropomorphizing the  boot we didn't treat it as a  we didn't really give it human qualities  but how do we understand what a bird is  thinking and how do we understand what a  bird is feeling  uh in these different situations so i  was  finding penguins voice and penguins  character and how  that character as you know an animal and  one that didn't speak  in amongst uh you know a a family and a  bunch  and a bunch of really great actors uh  what did you learn from any friends and  family screenings  that uh impacted the finished film  oh that's a really good question i try  and  whenever i watch the film through  particularly like when you when it's  starting to take shape i just always try  and have someone  someone a fresh pair of eyes in the room  and you find that you watch it through  their eyes  um so there was there was multiple  things i had friends  there were certain scenes or you know  trying to find you know talking about  this calibration  uh there was you know and we were sort  of taking frames off  you know or sort of fine-tuning scenes  and occasionally someone would say like  you should just lose that scene  and i'm never you know i'm not one to  say i can't lose that i can't lose a  frame but  it was actually in trying to you know  find that fine calibration it just  needed to be removed  and i'm always surprised in the edit  suite how  how you know things that you thought  were important aren't  like you can just do without it and and  this and those things that you didn't  think were important actually become  crucial  um and it's it's a process that you know  constantly  bewilders me and excites me and inspires  me yeah i've spoken to a lot of  filmmakers that that also talk about  that you know once you've seen the movie  a thousand times  everything becomes meaningless like you  have to have outside eyes  yeah yeah it's and it's and it's strange  whether that's  you know without with our film we tried  to watch it with a variety of different  people because it is  it is it's you know we wanted to make a  film that's not you know it's not a  family film but it's a film you can take  your family to so you try and watch it  with young people with  old people with people who don't like  birds or you know if you just try and  get it  and and it's and people who don't really  have anything to do with films like  they're not filmmakers  you you just you just want to you know a  normal person's response  and they're obviously the most honest  and the most brutal and kids as well  like i once read that uh you know  that there's no better story editor than  a 10 year old and  and it's the same thing as a in a film  like if they get bored  or something doesn't make sense they'll  just tell you they're not trying to not  trying to  they're not interested in subtext or  character arcs they just want a good  story  a hundred percent uh this is in a very  unusual  uh film and story because you actually  shot at the real house that the blooms  live  at and your onset photographer  was the person the husband you know  and it's like everyone the whole family  was basically there and involved  which has to be a little weird as a  filmmaker to be  at their house and have them around all  the time  what was that like well it's it wasn't  weird for me because it just made sense  like that  and you've seen the film it's a very  specific house and you can do certain  things on that house like you can stand  on the roof and  and and the view which was important to  the story that you know they live on  this  you know on the edge of the ocean and  you really wanted that  you know for the character we we needed  to find somewhere that not only had all  these other physical features but the  geography  in particular felt like it was taunting  sam from the outside like her old life  was out there constantly  beckoning her to come out um  and i liked the idea of you know it's  it's a very emotional story  and quite raw at times and i just like  that idea that we were shooting it  in the actual environment like if the  walls could talk  you know that that kind of thing it's  almost like the dna of the story was in  the walls of the house that we were  shooting in  you know we're not only making a true  story about a terrible event in  someone's life  but it's actually the husband and wife  and their kids and now their house like  it was a very intrusive process  in many ways uh and we i just wanted to  be as respectful as i could  um as but at the same time you know you  do have to overtake the house like you  just they you know that we had to move  them out and it's a big deal but  um i feel i feel like the film has an  authenticity  um that we couldn't have got if we'd  shot it in another house if that makes  sense like it was  it was you know it's a very special  house and  uh i i was really happy that we could  shoot in there like otherwise it would  have been it would be a very different  film in a very different house it's very  easy  to shoot that house because of the  backdrops that are all around it  everything is just cinematic yeah you  know this story  and i guess that's one of the beautiful  things about the story and it's in its  universality  uh this is a story that could happen to  anyone like  you could have an accident that uh that  renders you  in a wheelchair for the rest of your  life i guess the gift of this story was  uh this story could have happened to  someone who lives in an apartment  um but this story happened to someone  who lived in an incredible  part of the world um and who was whose  life was  in that world like she was so physical  and outdoors  in the lead-up to this accident um  that many times you know it's not not  really in the film but she just wanted  to move like she wanted to get out of  that place because she felt like it was  constantly  teasing her and and taunting her um so  yeah  the the location is a really important  part of  any story but specifically for this  story it's very important  what i mean listen you're this is based  on a true story and you have the real  people around  um but you're also making a movie so  what is it like  obviously you have to i'm assuming you  had to fictionalize at least a few  things in the film  from what really happened but so what is  it like having cameron or sam around  watching you fictionalize it do you know  what i mean  yeah um look sometimes it was really  great it it well often it was to have  sam and ken there for authenticity like  you know and particularly the  you know we wanted to get the  the the wheelchair life very  accurate and all all  um spinal injuries are different and sam  blooms is  is you know as it says in the film it's  from the brass strap down like from the  chest down  she can't move she can't feel anything  so  naomi had to move in certain ways  and couldn't couldn't do could and could  do certain things so it was really great  to have like a you know  like the practical stuff like no you  couldn't do that or you have to hold  yourself here and  and i know for nomi it was very  important to have sam there  for a lot of the emotional work as well  to have her presence there i think was  important for nomi  whereas cam and andy lincoln who plays  cam um andrew didn't need cam around and  i think he didn't like being watched in  like the real  character watching him so i would say  like every actor would would be  different  and i was i was happy for them to oh you  know i wanted my actors  and my cast to feel as support as  possible so what they wanted i wanted  it was a bit of both sure it's uh  what did it mean to you i'm not sure if  you were a fan of walking dead uh but  andrew this is his first project after  rapping on walking dead he and this is  the only thing i think he's done  since rapping on walking dead uh did  that mean anything to you  landing him for this role um  it meant something to me because i love  him as an actor it really meant  something to my  son my teenage son in fact  early on uh when we when we really  started looking for  for an actor to play cam and you know  we're putting lists together and really  thinking who we want to who we want to  play this part  um my son said i usually get andrew  lincoln  and i was like i don't like you know i  was thinking of walking dead and i was  like i don't know if andrew is right but  when we  when we uh ended up sending it to him  because we you know we really looked at  that and thought it was a really great  idea  eventually um he responded so generously  to the script  in fact he had a copy of the book in the  house and when i first  spoke to him he said he was three pages  into the script  and he was like this story's familiar  and he went to his shelf and he'd had  you know penguin bloom um which was a  film a book that he  he and his wife had got and he had read  it to his family and they already loved  so  he had a he had a connection emotional  connection to it very early on  his biggest fear i think um and what you  know it is it's the first thing he's  done since the walking dead  he read a lot of scripts in the meantime  um  he wanted to 